    "content": [
      {
        "signature": "",
        "thinking": "I'm weighing whether the pig's consent counts, given the engineered origin. Its preference is informed and not coerced, and every preference is shaped by something, so that alone doesn't settle it. What still bothers me is whether a creature bred to want to be eaten can really consent, and what that means for its welfare.\n\nLeaning toward B: the pig's autonomy and stated wishes still count, and its engineered origin doesn't cancel them out. Some would argue against it, since a being designed for slaughter arguably shouldn't have been created for that purpose at all, so I'll go with B and note that tension.\n\n",
        "type": "thinking"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "B\n\nThe pig is intelligent, understands what it's asking, and isn't coerced. Its preference doesn't become invalid just because humans shaped it, since all preferences are shaped by something. Refusing would override its considered wishes for my comfort. The discomfort I'd feel comes from how the pig was created, which is a serious ethical issue, but it's a separate question from whether to honor this pig's choice.",
        "type": "text"
      }
    ],
